--- !u!114 &257668335
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 257668334}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 6201163bca0816e4ca045707ac352343, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ArtifactData
  artifactTitle: Ancient Clay Storage Jar
  artifactDescription: 'The Ancient Clay Storage Jar is a well-preserved ceramic
    vessel dating to approximately the 5th century CE. It was discovered during a
    systematic archaeological excavation beneath the remains of a residential structure,
    where it had been partially buried in the floor of what is believed to have been
    a household storage area. Archaeologists believe the jar was used to store essential
    food supplies such as grains, rice, dried legumes, oils, and water, helping families
    preserve resources for daily consumption and periods of scarcity. Its large capacity
    and sturdy construction suggest it played an important role in the everyday lives
    of the settlement''s inhabitants.


    The vessel was hand-shaped using locally
    sourced clay before being fired in a traditional kiln. Close examination of its
    surface reveals subtle horizontal ridges and faint finger impressions left by
    the artisan during the shaping process, providing valuable evidence of the pottery-making
    techniques practiced at the time. The jar''s wide body, rounded base, and narrow
    neck were intentionally designed to maximize storage capacity while reducing
    contamination from dust, insects, and moisture. Although simple in appearance,
    the craftsmanship demonstrates a high level of skill and knowledge of ceramic
    production.


    Unlike decorative ceremonial pottery, this storage jar shows
    clear signs of regular household use. Wear patterns around the rim indicate frequent
    opening and closing, while traces of organic residue found inside the vessel
    suggest it once contained cereal grains and plant-based oils. Scientific analysis
    of microscopic residue has helped researchers better understand the agricultural
    practices, dietary habits, and food preservation methods of the ancient community.
    The discovery of similar jars throughout the excavation site indicates that standardized
    pottery production and organized food storage were common among the settlement''s
    residents.


    Despite spending more than 1,500 years underground, the jar
    remains remarkably intact, with only minor surface cracks and edge chipping caused
    by natural soil pressure over time. Conservation specialists carefully cleaned,
    stabilized, and restored the artifact using modern preservation techniques to
    prevent further deterioration while maintaining its original appearance. Today,
    the Ancient Clay Storage Jar serves as an important archaeological artifact,
    offering valuable insight into the domestic life, technological knowledge, and
    economic practices of the people who once inhabited the region. It stands as
    a testament to the ingenuity and craftsmanship of ancient potters and highlights
    the significance of pottery in sustaining everyday life throughout history.'
  originalOrigin: {fileID: 257668333}
